 "transcript": {
  "answer": "**Perth's electricity prices are rising more slowly than elsewhere in Australia, so a big bill probably isn't from tariff increases.** Your bill may still feel large because of everything else getting dearer.\n\n**Electricity prices, ABS CPI, August 2026, year on year**\n- Perth: **+2.4%**. The index is 102.4, up from 100.0 in August 2025.\n- Australia (eight-capital average): **+13.2%**. The index is 113.7, up from 100.5.\n\nPerth's rises have been small and steady. The index was +2.5% year on year from April to June 2026, then +2.4% after the July reset. WA sets household tariffs by regulation, so they're stable. Much of the rest of the country has been hit by the end of the 2024 rebates and by higher market-linked prices.\n\n**Why your bill may still feel huge**\n- **Overall Perth inflation is high.** The all-groups CPI is **+4.3%** year on year in August 2026, slightly above the national **+4.0%**. Other costs are squeezing your budget too.\n- **Prices are higher than a few years ago.** Perth's index was about 90.6 in early 2022 and is 102.4 now, roughly 13% higher.\n- **Usage matters.** Perth's long, hot summers and air conditioning can push up a bill whatever the tariff does.\n\n**Caveat on the Perth data**\nThe Perth monthly electricity series swings wildly. It dropped to 14.2 in mid-2022 and 25.7 in early 2025, then rose by up to 289% year on year in some months. Those swings come from government energy rebate credits being counted when they were paid. They don't reflect real price changes. The comparisons above (April–August, with no credits in play) are the reliable ones. If you got a rebate credit in the past, you may now be paying the full amount, which would feel like a jump even though the tariff only rose about 2.4%.\n\nComparing your last few bills for usage (kWh) and the daily supply charge would show whether the cause is how much you're using or the rates.",
